nce i started watching and keeping up  and then i had the pleasure of meeting  you in athens and andes  or desi and just  i believed in what you're doing and  i've been involved with a ministry in  athens that  [Music]  tries to take race out of everything  we're doing and it is  a ministry that has a school that all  started with football and  but just a heart for the people that are  having to live in  conditions that just don't really seem  right that no one should have to do that  and especially the children that's  that's what really is is heartbreaking  to watch so  excuse me just seeing what you're doing  is just amazing and i wanted to have  some small part in it and just get to  know everything better yeah thank you  one of the things you said that i want  to ask you about is that you said that  it started as a result of football  what was the context of that i know you  all started school k through five  and it was started  not  out of a school it was started first  from football can you explain that  because i think people need to hear the  context the background and how that came  about especially since we're talking a  little bit about race right well i want  to give credit where credit's due and  there's a local pastor there hal  farnsworth at redeemer presbyterian  i think wrestled with the thought  of knowing about the poverty and and all  the problems  in clark county where athens is located  and primarily uh  in the black community of just people  just struggling through poverty so bad  he wanted to do something and  so  his question was how can i bring the  black  community together with white community  some common bond and he decided football  was it and he started a program  uh for football called  the downtown falcons and  that started off with a few kids and  just kept growing and and now that part  of it's up over  i think 300  children that actually play football  and then from there  the ministry decided we need  to give these children excuse me another  children  a school opportunity and just  uh  something that they could get some  stability in their lives and  and some direction and love and and so  many things so  football became school and and now we've  taken it even further to include  a program called our daily bread that  where we're feeding people every day and  so  that football started  starting everything there you know  that's really important because  we're living in a day in time especially  in chicago where  people want to think that a lot of our  problems are a result of racial issues  and i beg to differ  and i think all the problems that we're  faced with  if we all work together and i say this a  lot about being neighborly if we can  just be neighborly there's absolutely  nothing  on that we can't overcome whether it's  giving food to people who need it  creating education  i know for you you've been a mentor and  that's we're really big on that there  are so many young people  in chicago  who need positive mentors and  need life change and i've been able to  witness for myself  how positive and strong it is when you  have a mentor regardless of  their color and i wish we had time we  would talk about how you're a mentor to  a kid of another race and  how important that is you know but just  tonight that you're here on the south  side of chicago  and  we're talking about the issues and  we're praying about the solutions uh  speaks volumes and i hope that america  can be just like we are tonight yeah one  yeah well and i think we all want  somebody to believe in us and we want to  know that somebody cares and that's what  i saw with these children  when football became a basketball  program some of the  kids i had the most trouble with on my  team just really want some attention  they wanted to know that i loved them  and i cared about them and  and that goes all the way from childhood  to adulthood and  so i think you're right we you asked me  a while ago uh  what my word  of this meeting was and and i said grace  but  i think we all need grace towards each  other  we're all a challenge we're all a mess  in our own way and  and it's tough to love each other  sometimes but  that's what we're supposed to do i mean  it's it's very plain you can easily read  that  you know i tell people all the time that  government  can make laws  but they can't  change hearts  and  it's god and people  yeah that work on the hearts  and so i want to thank you for what  you're doing in athens georgia thank you  for coming all the way to chicago i'll  be back i hope our fox family uh follows  you and uh please remember  projecthood.org where we're building  this center on the south side of chicago  to help as many people as we can to stop  as much violence as we possibly can  we need your help and your assistance  and uh we're gonna keep coming to you  every day talking about these fireside  chats these rooftop revelations so i'll  see you tomorrow night  god bless you and god thank you  god bless amir  you

